Cluster Reading — data model proposal (escalation #1682, follow-on)

§7 Naming inventory

'cluster_reading' is too long as prefix for the tables.  cluster is only part of the collection, not the collection. reading is only part of the operation, not the only operation.  The entire operation is about inner being observations and the end result is the a collection of all the findings. IB_obs as a prefix for the tables would be preferred.

'trace' - is this the right word?  it is not a pure index - because this would imply it is a FK connector, which is only partly true. it is like the footprint of each observation, but it is more than a impression in the sand, it connects, show relevance. Its entire existance is to be a node, a point of connection in the spiderweb of related cords.  each node map to other nodes directly or indirectly through the cord attachments. Each column in the table is another cord.  I think trace should be replaced by IB_node 

'family' - the family in the context of it usage is a group of terms with underlying commonality that is loosely coupled.  Its main purpose is to work as a sub-division of a cluster, which is a similar grouping of terms but with a courser grain of similarity.  Both of these grouping is neither scientific or precise, it is an approximation and it is highly likely that the contextual analysis of the terms will cause meaning to cross boundaries of cluster and family.  Alternative terms used in the study that means the same thing more or less is VCG verse context group, cluster sub group, phenomena, characteristic - all in different grain of the same concept. There are differences, especially in how the commonality is derived at.  VSG looks at the verse context, and is closer to a characteristic than a family; on the other hand cluster subgroup is derived in exactly the same way as family.  family on the other hand is a label that have a lot of different meanings.  My preferance is to revert back to cluster_subgroup. its current definition in the study is true to what family stands for, and there is no reason to introduce another competing term.

'observation' - observation is used for all the individual statements about a particular node. it describes something that can be traced back through the IB_node cords (columns) to its roots. It is not necessarily a fact,  or proven, it may be a pointer, signpost, or reminder for further exploration.  Other words used in the same vain is findings, facts, pointers, debate and others.  This study started by referencing it as findings, and migrated towards observations, largely because findings are narrower in definition while observations are more tollerable for what it can include.  Over time it was observed that the inner being characteristic are not exact, difficult to define, have many dimensions and have lots that is highly debatable. I prefer to stick with observations.

'tag' vs 'kind - both words, plus some more have been used to convey the same concept. other words in the study for the same include flag, and in some places 'type', 'category', 'sub_category' - all equally unhelpful.  all of these label something.  I prefer tag above kind, maybe because tag is more commonly used and kind only started to creep in via the lexical analysis terminology.

"verse" vs "reference" vs "osisId" vs "verse_reference" : the verse table use reference as the canonical reference in the bible for the verse.  verse.reference is easily understood as the reference on the verse table.  the IB_node table should use verse_reference to be explicit to what is it referring to.  However, there is a deeper issue - potentially.  I am not sure that the formatting of the verse referencing in the observations and other text are consistent. I think it is all over the place. I do remember a major effort to ensure that book names, and references are normalised.  This must absolutely be inforced accross the entire corpus, especially as verse-references are commonly used for tracing and searching.

"synthesis_id" vs "stable_key" - this, in itself begs a question. is this naming issue purely a factor of resolving convention conflict in prototyping results?  will this naming convension live outside the JSONs.  I can imagine you need a numbering system for each JSON (and there will be many to come) and for each observation in the json. that need to be specified in the script that generates the json. Terminology for these references must not be generic so it gets confused with references outside the json.

"span_surface"/"span_morph" vs `span.surface`/`span.morph_code`; this consideration is all about naming convention and sticking to the same convention. span.surface reference the column surface in the table span.  if one refer the to the same column in the ib_node table it would be ib_node.surface.  if you thinking if there is a requirement to name the column surface in the ib_node table, then it should not be span_surface but surface.  there is only one source for surface and that is span.surface therefore using surface in ib_node does not have to be expanded to span_surface.  this convention should apply throughout. do not name the same column differently in every table, unless the table itself have a naming conflict.

table columns:  I need more information on the following columns to determine if they are appropriately named: stage, slant_label, statement, status, revisit note, seq

other names: the sundry cluster family: SUNDRY - not acceptable.  We already have a convention for similar out of place items - it is FLAG. in real terms these are terms that does not fit, and need to be considered for reallocation to the FLAG cluster.  however, inside the family generation process, it will only signpost it to the FLAG family, rather than action it to be reallocated.  
Most of the confusion in §7.5 is because you are not inspecting the tables that you are relating to, and not using the specific columns you are referring to. sort that out first.
You have a tendency of inventing short codes that refer to previous documents that does not survive, or sometimes change. Use descriptive nomenclature, not just a code.
